@*
    The Settings page's Appearance tab: pick a theme from ThemeCatalog.Grouped.

    A theme carries exactly one palette and its own ThemeMode, so picking a theme picks light or dark
    with it - there is no separate light/dark control any more, and so no way to ask for "Solarized
    Dark, in light mode" and be shown a different theme's palette instead
    (docs/adr/0017-a-theme-is-a-palette-not-a-pair.md). The Human's own Avatar used to be edited here;
    it moved to the Profile tab (ProfilePanel), with the rest of who the Human is.

    A MudList rather than a MudSelect: MudBlazor 9 has no MudSelectItemGroup, so a MudSelect cannot
    render the Light / Dark / High contrast headings this picker is grouped by. The list also shows
    every theme at once, which is what a theme picker wants - the same shape VS Code's own picker has,
    and the catalog these themes came from.

    The grouping is ThemeCatalog.Grouped's, computed once there rather than here, because
    ThemeCatalog.BuiltIn's ORDER is load-bearing - three call sites read BuiltIn[0] as "the default
    theme" - so the picker must never be tempted to reorder the catalog to group it.

    MudBlazor's MudThemeProvider lives in the render tree (MainLayout.razor), so choosing a theme here
    applies immediately through that component's own parameters - no page reload, no <head> to
    rewrite, no JavaScript interop of this component's own.

    Subscribes to AppearanceStore.AppearanceChanged the same way Settings.razor subscribes to
    PromptStore.PromptsChanged: the store is a singleton, so a hand-edit to appearance.json (or a save from another browser
    tab) must repaint this tab without a restart, and rules.md's "unsubscribe in Dispose" reasoning
    for RoomEvents applies here for the same reason - a leaked subscription to a singleton never
    dies.
*@

@using Agency.Huddle.App.Appearance
@using Agency.Huddle.App.Themes
@implements IDisposable
@inject AppearanceStore AppearanceStore

<SettingsIntro>
    Pick one light theme and one dark theme. The button at the right of the top bar switches between
    them.
</SettingsIntro>

<StoredAt Label="The selection is stored at" Path="@this.AppearanceStore.FilePath" />

<MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle2">Light theme</MudText>
<MudList T="string"
         Class="theme-picker"
         SelectionMode="SelectionMode.SingleSelection"
         SelectedValue="@this.AppearanceStore.Current.PreferredLightId"
         SelectedValueChanged="this.OnThemeChangedAsync"
         aria-label="Light theme">
    @foreach (var grouping in GroupedFor(ThemeMode.Light))
    {
        <MudListSubheader Class="theme-picker-heading">@grouping.Heading</MudListSubheader>
        @foreach (var theme in grouping.Themes)
        {
            <MudListItem T="string" Value="@theme.Id" Text="@theme.Label" />
        }
    }
</MudList>

<MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle2" Class="mt-4">Dark theme</MudText>
<MudList T="string"
         Class="theme-picker"
         SelectionMode="SelectionMode.SingleSelection"
         SelectedValue="@this.AppearanceStore.Current.PreferredDarkId"
         SelectedValueChanged="this.OnThemeChangedAsync"
         aria-label="Dark theme">
    @foreach (var grouping in GroupedFor(ThemeMode.Dark))
    {
        <MudListSubheader Class="theme-picker-heading">@grouping.Heading</MudListSubheader>
        @foreach (var theme in grouping.Themes)
        {
            <MudListItem T="string" Value="@theme.Id" Text="@theme.Label" />
        }
    }
</MudList>

@if (this.IsCustomAccentSelected)
{
    @* THE TRAP applies here exactly as it does to the avatar's MudColorPicker below: bind Text, never
       Value - Value is a non-null MudColor, so binding it would materialise a hard-coded hex colour
       the instant this tab first renders a Custom Accent selection with no colour chosen yet. Shows
       the colour exactly as picked; AccentTheme.BuildTheme is what clamps it for contrast when it
       renders, so what MainLayout paints can differ slightly from what is shown here - by design, see
       AccentPalette's own remarks. *@
    <MudColorPicker Label="Accent colour" ColorPickerMode="ColorPickerMode.HEX" ShowAlpha="false"
                     Text="@this.AccentColorText" TextChanged="this.OnAccentColorTextChanged"
                     Class="mt-2 theme-picker-accent-colour" />
}

<p class="settings-theme-credit">
    Custom Accent lets you pick your own accent colour, adjusted if needed so it stays readable.
    Every other theme except Huddle Light and Huddle Dark is one of the colour themes bundled with Visual Studio Code, mapped onto this application's palette. Visual Studio Code and its default
    themes are Microsoft's, under the MIT licence; Solarized is Ethan Schoonover's and Monokai is
    Wimer Hazenberg's.
</p>

<p class="settings-prompts-file-path">
    Your own avatar is set on the <a href="/settings/profile">Profile</a> tab.
</p>

@code {
    /// <summary><see cref="ThemeCatalog.Grouped"/> narrowed to the themes of one <see cref="ThemeMode"/>, so each picker lists only themes that can fill its slot; "High contrast" appears under both.</summary>
    /// <param name="mode">The mode the picker's slot holds.</param>
    private static IEnumerable<ThemeGrouping> GroupedFor(ThemeMode mode)
    {
        return ThemeCatalog.Grouped
            .Select(grouping => grouping with { Themes = [.. grouping.Themes.Where(theme => theme.Mode == mode)] })
            .Where(grouping => grouping.Themes.Count > 0);
    }

    /// <summary>Whether the colour picker should show at all: only when one of the two <see cref="AccentTheme"/> entries is selected, since every other theme ignores <see cref="AppearanceSettings.AccentColorHex"/> entirely.</summary>
    private bool IsCustomAccentSelected =>
        string.Equals(this.AppearanceStore.Current.PreferredLightId, AccentTheme.LightDescriptor.Id, StringComparison.Ordinal) ||
        string.Equals(this.AppearanceStore.Current.PreferredDarkId, AccentTheme.DarkDescriptor.Id, StringComparison.Ordinal);

    /// <summary>The accent colour shown in the picker: the store's own value, read straight through with no local buffer - since there is only one value here and nothing to seed across a mode switch the way the avatar editor's fields need.</summary>
    private string? AccentColorText => this.AppearanceStore.Current.AccentColorHex;

    /// <summary>Subscribes to <see cref="AppearanceStore.AppearanceChanged"/> - see the file-level comment for why this matters.</summary>
    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        this.AppearanceStore.AppearanceChanged += this.OnAppearanceChanged;
    }

    /// <summary>Unsubscribes from <see cref="AppearanceStore.AppearanceChanged"/> - see the file-level comment for why this matters.</summary>
    public void Dispose()
    {
        this.AppearanceStore.AppearanceChanged -= this.OnAppearanceChanged;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Saves the newly picked theme as the Human's light or dark theme, whichever its mode is. A null
    /// reaches here when MudList clears its selection, which SingleSelection does when the selected
    /// item is clicked again; it is ignored, so a slot always holds a theme.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="themeId">The theme id the Human just picked.</param>
    private Task OnThemeChangedAsync(string? themeId)
    {
        if (themeId is not null)
        {
            this.AppearanceStore.SavePreferred(themeId);
        }

        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Saves the newly picked accent colour, mirroring <see cref="OnThemeChangedAsync"/> one key over.
    /// Blank input (the picker's Clear action, or a Human who deletes the hex text) maps to
    /// <see langword="null"/>, meaning "no accent colour chosen" - the same convention
    /// <see cref="AvatarEditor"/> uses for the avatar's own colour picker.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="value">The colour picker's newly typed or picked text, or <see langword="null"/>/blank.</param>
    private void OnAccentColorTextChanged(string? value)
    {
        this.AppearanceStore.SaveAccentColor(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(value) ? null : value);
    }

    private void OnAppearanceChanged()
    {
        _ = RendererDispatch.RunAsync(this.InvokeAsync, () =>
        {
            this.StateHasChanged();
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        });
    }
}
